 Try a rule offline with `harness ttsr test --rule <file> --source tool --tool write --path <path> -- '<snippet>'`.
 
+### Compaction from the workflow record
+
+When Coding Harness compacts a session with an active workflow, the adapter
+writes the summary instead of a model. It runs `validate-state` as Claude Code's
+PreCompact hook does, then builds the summary from the record on disk:
+
+- the human's last three messages from the part being compacted;
+- the position from `aidlc-state.md` (phase, current stage and its gate state);
+- the directive in flight and the command that re-issues it, since compaction
+  clears the parts already delivered;
+- the human-approved artifacts, open questions (empty `[Answer]:` tags) and the
+  last three human decisions from the audit log;
+- the files the compacted part of the session wrote or edited;
+- the engine's own post-compaction context (what Claude Code injects through
+  SessionStart `source=compact`).
+
+With no active workflow, an unreadable record, or focus text (`/compact <focus>`),
+the harness writes its usual summary.
+
+A turn that moves the workflow to another stage compacts once context use
+reaches 40%, so each stage starts with a summary rather than a full transcript.
+Set the threshold in `aidlc.settings.json`; `0` turns it off:
+
+```json
+{ "harness": { "schemaVersion": 1, "compactAtStageBoundary": 60 } }
+```
+
 ## Alongside Claude Code
 
 One project can carry both distributions: run `aidlc config --harness claude`
